1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,700
Two thousand one hundred thirteen dollars.

2
00:00:02,860 --> 00:00:07,050
That's the federal tax on the next ten
thousand dollars a retired couple takes

3
00:00:07,100 --> 00:00:08,100
out of an I R A…

4
00:00:08,640 --> 00:00:10,020
in the twelve percent bracket.

5
00:00:10,620 --> 00:00:11,660
Not twelve hundred.

6
00:00:11,710 --> 00:00:12,710
Twenty-one hundred.

7
00:00:13,540 --> 00:00:15,900
And the extra nine hundred isn't on the I
R A.

8
00:00:16,420 --> 00:00:17,680
It's on their Social Security.

9
00:00:18,840 --> 00:00:20,260
This is Run the Number.

10
00:00:20,880 --> 00:00:25,310
By the end you'll know where the zone
starts, where it ends, and the one line

11
00:00:25,360 --> 00:00:27,810
on your return that says whether you're
standing in it.

12
00:00:27,860 --> 00:00:30,320
This is education, not tax advice.

13
00:00:30,880 --> 00:00:33,160
The couple is an example, not a real
person.

14
00:00:34,340 --> 00:00:35,340
The rule first.

15
00:00:36,140 --> 00:00:38,720
Social Security was tax-free until
nineteen eighty-three.

16
00:00:39,620 --> 00:00:44,540
That year Congress wrote a line: add your
other income, half your Social Security,

17
00:00:44,780 --> 00:00:46,460
and any tax-exempt interest.

18
00:00:46,980 --> 00:00:49,260
The I R S calls it combined income.

19
00:00:49,880 --> 00:00:53,990
Above thirty-two thousand dollars for a
couple, up to half of the benefit becomes

20
00:00:54,040 --> 00:00:54,980
taxable.

21
00:00:55,030 --> 00:00:59,970
In nineteen ninety-three a second line was
added: above forty-four thousand, up

22
00:01:00,020 --> 00:01:01,450
to eighty-five percent.

23
00:01:01,500 --> 00:01:02,500
Two numbers.

24
00:01:03,200 --> 00:01:04,300
Thirty-two thousand.

25
00:01:04,350 --> 00:01:05,500
Forty-four thousand.

26
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:07,980
They have never been adjusted for
inflation.

27
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:10,420
Not once in forty-three years.

28
00:01:11,400 --> 00:01:13,660
Benefits go up with the cost of living
every January.

29
00:01:14,180 --> 00:01:16,800
The lines stay where Reagan and Clinton
left them.

30
00:01:17,520 --> 00:01:22,240
In nineteen eighty-four, fewer than one
retiree in ten paid tax on a benefit.

31
00:01:22,840 --> 00:01:24,440
Today it's close to half.

32
00:01:25,560 --> 00:01:26,880
How to check yours.

33
00:01:27,260 --> 00:01:29,260
Last year's return, line six.

34
00:01:29,310 --> 00:01:32,340
Six a is the Social Security you received.

35
00:01:32,960 --> 00:01:34,940
Six b is the part that got taxed.

36
00:01:35,440 --> 00:01:38,040
If six b is zero, you're under the first
line.

37
00:01:38,500 --> 00:01:42,060
If six b is eighty-five percent of six a,
you're past the second.

38
00:01:42,560 --> 00:01:43,560
Anywhere in between…

39
00:01:44,140 --> 00:01:45,140
you're in the zone.

40
00:01:47,280 --> 00:01:48,280
Now the couple.

41
00:01:48,420 --> 00:01:49,010
Both sixty-seven.

42
00:01:49,060 --> 00:01:54,010
Forty thousand dollars a year in Social
Security between them, and an I R A they

43
00:01:54,060 --> 00:01:55,120
draw on for the rest.

44
00:01:55,880 --> 00:01:56,920
Twenty twenty-seven numbers,

45
00:01:57,660 --> 00:01:59,760
married filing jointly, no state tax.

46
00:02:00,460 --> 00:02:02,500
Their deductions, from the last episode:

47
00:02:03,340 --> 00:02:04,400
forty-eight thousand six hundred.

48
00:02:05,360 --> 00:02:07,950
Start the withdrawals at zero and run them
up.

49
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:11,320
The first twelve thousand out of the I R A
costs nothing.

50
00:02:12,240 --> 00:02:15,240
Combined income is thirty-two thousand,
right at the first line.

51
00:02:15,820 --> 00:02:20,310
From twelve to twenty-four thousand, every
dollar out drags fifty cents of Social

52
00:02:20,360 --> 00:02:22,220
Security into the taxable column.

53
00:02:22,580 --> 00:02:26,200
Past twenty-four thousand, every dollar
drags eighty-five cents.

54
00:02:26,640 --> 00:02:30,040
And still no tax, because the deductions
are soaking it all up.

55
00:02:30,540 --> 00:02:34,290
The first dollar of federal tax lands at
thirty-four thousand fifty-five dollars

56
00:02:34,340 --> 00:02:35,720
out of the I R A.

57
00:02:36,200 --> 00:02:37,520
That part is good news.

58
00:02:38,080 --> 00:02:39,560
Thirty-four thousand, free.

59
00:02:40,260 --> 00:02:41,580
Then the meter turns on.

60
00:02:41,920 --> 00:02:47,030
From thirty-four thousand to about
thirty-seven, each dollar out is taxed at
ten

61
00:02:47,080 --> 00:02:51,030
percent, and so are the eighty-five cents
of Social Security it dragged in with

62
00:02:51,080 --> 00:02:51,750
it.

63
00:02:51,800 --> 00:02:53,800
Eighteen and a half cents on the dollar.

64
00:02:54,440 --> 00:02:58,680
Cross into the twelve percent bracket and
it becomes twenty-two point two cents.

65
00:02:59,380 --> 00:03:01,130
Twelve percent of a dollar eighty-five.

66
00:03:01,180 --> 00:03:03,200
Run it.

67
00:03:03,700 --> 00:03:05,480
Forty-five thousand out of the I R A.

68
00:03:06,380 --> 00:03:09,600
Taxable Social Security: twenty-three
thousand eight hundred fifty.

69
00:03:10,500 --> 00:03:14,300
Taxable income after the deductions:
twenty thousand two hundred fifty.

70
00:03:15,300 --> 00:03:16,800
Tax: two thousand twenty-five.

71
00:03:17,600 --> 00:03:19,480
Now fifty-five thousand out.

72
00:03:20,100 --> 00:03:21,100
Ten thousand more.

73
00:03:21,540 --> 00:03:25,020
Taxable Social Security climbs to
thirty-two thousand three hundred fifty.

74
00:03:25,940 --> 00:03:28,860
Taxable income: thirty-eight thousand
seven hundred fifty.

75
00:03:29,780 --> 00:03:31,660
Tax: four thousand one hundred
thirty-eight.

76
00:03:32,440 --> 00:03:33,900
The ten thousand dollars cost…

77
00:03:34,520 --> 00:03:36,040
two thousand one hundred thirteen.

78
00:03:36,820 --> 00:03:38,200
In the twelve percent bracket.

79
00:03:38,940 --> 00:03:39,980
Here's the strange part.

80
00:03:40,580 --> 00:03:41,470
Keep going.

81
00:03:41,520 --> 00:03:45,950
At about fifty-seven thousand out,
eighty-five percent of the benefit is
taxable

82
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:48,180
and the rule has nothing left to take.

83
00:03:48,600 --> 00:03:49,860
The torpedo is over.

84
00:03:50,460 --> 00:03:52,550
The next ten thousand, from fifty-seven to

85
00:03:52,600 --> 00:03:55,360
sixty-seven, costs twelve hundred dollars.

86
00:03:55,410 --> 00:03:56,410
Twelve cents.

87
00:03:57,060 --> 00:04:01,410
Same couple, same bracket, and the dollar
after the zone is cheaper than the dollar

88
00:04:01,460 --> 00:04:02,460
inside it.

89
00:04:02,520 --> 00:04:06,670
For this couple the zone runs from
thirty-four thousand to fifty-seven
thousand

90
00:04:06,720 --> 00:04:10,270
of withdrawals, and living in the middle
of it costs about three thousand seven

91
00:04:10,320 --> 00:04:13,800
hundred dollars a year more than the
brackets alone would charge.

92
00:04:14,500 --> 00:04:15,520
Now the fine print.

93
00:04:15,920 --> 00:04:16,920
Three lines.

94
00:04:17,240 --> 00:04:17,950
One.

95
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:21,770
The six thousand dollar senior deduction
from the twenty twenty-five law doesn't

96
00:04:21,820 --> 00:04:22,820
move the lines.

97
00:04:23,500 --> 00:04:25,460
Combined income is figured before
deductions.

98
00:04:26,080 --> 00:04:30,160
So the deduction lowers the tax, but not
the share of the benefit that's taxable.

99
00:04:30,620 --> 00:04:32,880
The lines are still thirty-two and
forty-four.

100
00:04:33,560 --> 00:04:34,450
Two.

101
00:04:34,500 --> 00:04:36,240
Not every dollar counts the same.

102
00:04:36,680 --> 00:04:39,280
A Roth withdrawal is not in combined
income.

103
00:04:39,680 --> 00:04:43,990
A qualified charitable distribution,
straight from the I R A to a charity after

104
00:04:44,040 --> 00:04:46,160
seventy and a half, is not in it either.

105
00:04:46,800 --> 00:04:48,360
Tax-exempt bond interest is.

106
00:04:48,760 --> 00:04:50,360
That last one surprises people.

107
00:04:50,800 --> 00:04:54,500
The tax-free municipal bond raises the tax
on your Social Security.

108
00:04:55,200 --> 00:04:55,890
Three.

109
00:04:55,940 --> 00:04:57,520
The zone moves with the benefit.

110
00:04:58,120 --> 00:05:00,080
Bigger Social Security, longer zone.

111
00:05:00,540 --> 00:05:04,530
A single filer with fifty thousand a year
in benefits can run the zone into the

112
00:05:04,580 --> 00:05:09,210
twenty-two percent bracket, where the next
dollar costs forty point seven cents.

113
00:05:09,260 --> 00:05:11,340
Twenty-two percent of a dollar
eighty-five.

114
00:05:12,120 --> 00:05:13,120
How to check yours.

115
00:05:13,560 --> 00:05:18,730
If six b is more than zero and less than
eighty-five percent of six a, every extra

116
00:05:18,780 --> 00:05:22,960
I R A dollar this year is taxed at
eighteen and a half cents, or twenty-two.

117
00:05:23,500 --> 00:05:28,130
A fee-only fiduciary or a C P A, with your
return in front of them, can tell you

118
00:05:28,180 --> 00:05:31,640
how many dollars it takes to get out of
the zone, in either direction.

119
00:05:32,060 --> 00:05:33,060
Here's the pattern.

120
00:05:33,220 --> 00:05:35,000
The brackets are the sign on the road.

121
00:05:35,820 --> 00:05:37,100
Combined income is the road.

122
00:05:37,460 --> 00:05:41,670
A couple who knows where the zone starts
and ends can decide which year a big

123
00:05:41,720 --> 00:05:44,580
withdrawal lands in, and which account it
comes from.

124
00:05:45,100 --> 00:05:49,080
A couple who doesn't pays twenty-two cents
and reads twelve on the sign.

125
00:05:49,580 --> 00:05:53,890
On a nine hundred thousand dollar I R A,
that's a leak that never shows up on

126
00:05:53,940 --> 00:05:56,180
a statement, year after year.

127
00:05:56,980 --> 00:05:57,980
One thing this week.

128
00:05:58,460 --> 00:05:59,760
Find line six b.

129
00:06:00,300 --> 00:06:04,280
If it's between zero and eighty-five
percent of six a, you're in the zone.

130
00:06:04,900 --> 00:06:06,640
Write down what it would take to leave it.

131
00:06:07,980 --> 00:06:09,820
New numbers every Tuesday and Friday.

132
00:06:10,780 --> 00:06:12,320
Subscribe so the next one finds you.

133
00:06:12,820 --> 00:06:14,520
Will your money last as long as you do?

